<PageLayout
  title="MC1R — the pigment switch"
  kicker="Receptor · Melanocortin 1 receptor"
  description="The melanocortin 1 receptor gene (MC1R, chromosome 16q24.3) and the receptor on melanocytes that decides between protective brown-black eumelanin and reddish pheomelanin. Its weak variants explain red hair and fair skin — and carry an independent melanoma risk."
  thread="Pigment is the system made visible — the one receptor that turns a hormone into a shield, and the colour of a person into an act of protection."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Afamelanotide', type: 'Drug', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afamelanotide'] },
  ]}
>
  <ReceptorFacts receptor={receptor} />

  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'MC1R is the switch on melanocytes between protective brown-black eumelanin and reddish, poorly protective pheomelanin.',
      'α-MSH or ACTH activation (via Gs → cAMP → MITF) pushes toward eumelanin; weak signalling tips toward pheomelanin — red or blond hair, fair skin that burns, and freckling.',
      'Loss-of-function variants (classically R151C, R160W, D294H) raise cutaneous melanoma risk even after accounting for visible skin and hair colour.',
      'The MC1R agonist afamelanotide (Scenesse) is used to raise protective eumelanin in erythropoietic protoporphyria.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    MC1R is the melanocortin system's contribution to how you look — and to how
    well your skin defends itself against the sun. Sitting on the surface of
    melanocytes, it is the master switch that sets which kind of melanin those
    cells produce.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>The pigment switch</h2>
  <p>
    Melanocytes can make two broad classes of pigment: <strong>eumelanin</strong>,
    the brown-black pigment that absorbs UV and protects DNA, and
    <strong>pheomelanin</strong>, a reddish-yellow pigment that is a poor
    sunscreen and can itself generate damaging free radicals. When α-MSH (or ACTH)
    activates MC1R, the receptor — coupled to Gs — raises cyclic AMP, drives the
    pigment master-regulator MITF, and shifts production toward
    eumelanin.<Cite n={2} /> When MC1R signalling is weak, the balance tips toward
    pheomelanin: the visible result is red or blond hair, fair skin that burns
    rather than tans, and freckling.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The system also has a built-in brake. Agouti signalling protein antagonises
    MC1R, lowering its activity — the same mechanism that produces banded coat
    colour in other mammals.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <PigmentSwitch />

  <h2>The "red hair" variants</h2>
  <p>
    MC1R is one of the most variable genes in the human genome. A handful of
    strongly penetrant loss-of-function alleles — classically R151C, R160W, and
    D294H — are associated with the red-hair, fair-skin phenotype. These variants
    largely fail because the mutant receptor is mis-trafficked and under-expressed
    at the cell surface, so less signal gets through even when α-MSH is
    present.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>The MC1R gene</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>melanocortin 1 receptor gene</strong> — <em>MC1R</em> — sits on chromosome
    16 (locus 16q24.3) and is unusual among receptor genes in two ways: it is encoded by a
    single exon with no introns, and it is one of the most polymorphic genes in the human
    genome.<Cite n={4} /> Because the receptor's job is a binary pigment choice, each variant
    effectively re-tunes that switch. The classic "R" (red hair colour) alleles — strongly
    penetrant loss-of-function variants — are catalogued below; the{' '}
    <a href="/genetics">genetics reference</a> carries the full per-variant entries with
    evidence tags and dbSNP links.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b-2 border-[var(--border)] text-left align-bottom">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">MC1R gene variant</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">dbSNP</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Class</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Associated phenotype</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="align-top">
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">R151C</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/snp/rs1805007" rel="noopener">rs1805007 ↗</a></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]">R allele (strong loss-of-function)</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Red hair, fair skin, freckling; raised melanoma risk</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">R160W</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/snp/rs1805008" rel="noopener">rs1805008 ↗</a></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]">R allele (strong loss-of-function)</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Red hair, fair skin, freckling; raised melanoma risk</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">D294H</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/snp/rs1805009" rel="noopener">rs1805009 ↗</a></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]">R allele (strong loss-of-function)</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Red hair, fair skin, freckling; raised melanoma risk</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">D84E</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/snp/rs1805006" rel="noopener">rs1805006 ↗</a></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[var(--text)]">r allele (weaker effect)</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Fair skin, reduced tanning; modest risk contribution</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">R142H</td>
